<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">German Togoland</h1><p class="st-meta">1884 CE – 1916 CE · capital Lome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Germany's smallest African colony, promoted in Berlin as a model colony, which was taken by British and French forces in the first weeks of the First World War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>On 5 July 1884 the German envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-nachtigal">Gustav Nachtigal</a> signed a protection treaty with Mlapa III, a chief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togoville">Togoville</a> on Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> then pushed inland, fixing borders with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-dahomey">French Dahomey</a> in agreements of the 1890s. The capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lome</a> in 1897.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>German officials called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> a model colony because it covered its own costs. They built a wharf at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lome</a> and three railways, including the line to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kpalime">Palime</a> known as the cocoa railway, and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, cocoa and palm products. This was achieved through taxes, forced labour and harsh punishments. Catholic and Protestant missions ran most schools, and a large radio station was built at Kamina.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>British and French troops invaded in August 1914 and the Germans surrendered on 26 August. The territory was divided in 1916, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> later confirmed it as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-togoland">British Togoland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-togoland">French Togoland</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/german-togoland.jpg" alt="German Togoland" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Imperial Colonial Office of the German Empire Vector: David Liuzzo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reichskolonialflagge.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1884 CE – 1916 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Julius Graf von Zech</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Ewe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/togo">Togo</a>, <a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Togoland, Togo Protectorate, Schutzgebiet Togo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=german-togoland" aria-label="German Togoland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4714 1948 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where German Togoland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5034 2204L5000 2081M5034 2204L5013 2186M5034 2204L5300 622M5034 2204L4997 651M5034 2204L5034 2204"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5000 2081h0M5013 2186h0M5300 622h0M4997 651h0M5034 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5034 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5034 2204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lome. <a href="/atlas?year=1900&amp;focus=german-togoland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

